WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
Partial derivatives.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.503
Real models have millions of knobs.

3
00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:07.267
Here is how we ask each one which way to turn.

4
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:12.133
A partial derivative is a slope with respect to one variable.

5
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:14.100
We nudge just that one input.

6
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.333
Everything else holds perfectly still.

7
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.054
Then we watch how the function responds.

8
00:00:19.054 --> 00:00:21.233
The symbol is a curly d, called del.

9
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.433
Take a tiny model that predicts a planet's radius.

10
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:27.167
It has a weight, w.

11
00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:28.600
And a bias, b.

12
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.174
Both feed one loss.

13
00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:34.667
So the loss has two partial derivatives, one slope along each axis.

14
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:37.167
Here it is in Python.

15
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:41.267
One planet with mass two, and a true radius of one point four.

16
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:44.300
First we nudge only w, keeping b still.

17
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.967
Then we nudge only b.

18
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:49.738
The slopes are twenty two point four and eleven point two.

19
00:00:49.738 --> 00:00:51.533
Each knob gets its own answer.

20
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:54.300
Compare them side by side.

21
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.607
The loss is twice as sensitive to w as to b.

22
00:00:57.607 --> 00:01:01.367
Both are positive, so both knobs should be turned down.

23
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.410
Now scale up.

24
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.367
A light curve CNN has about four hundred thousand parameters.

25
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:14.500
One call to loss dot backward fills every parameter's grad with its own partial derivative.

26
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.833
When you inspect the gradient norm per layer to hunt vanishing gradients, you are reading statistics over these partials.

27
00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:23.778
Let's recap.

28
00:01:23.778 --> 00:01:27.067
A partial derivative is the slope along one variable.

29
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:28.933
All the others are held still.

30
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:30.867
Each weight gets its own slope.

31
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.457
And backward computes them all at once.

32
00:01:33.457 --> 00:01:34.867
Next up: the chain rule.
